.TH WERKMEISTER-TEST 1 "" "werkmeister" "Werkmeister Manual"
.SH NAME
werkmeister\-test \- Run Playwright tests against a feature's stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwerkmeister test\fR <feature> [extra...]
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-scaffold\fR
Don't run: write playwright.config.ts + \e<feature>.spec.ts into the workspace (if missing) and install the pinned @playwright/test
.TP
\fB\-w, \-\-workspace <path>\fR
\-\-scaffold: workspace directory (default: from cwd)
.TP
\fB\-\-base\-url <url>\fR
\-\-scaffold: ingress URL to target (default: the first feature_router service)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBWhat it does:\fR
.IP 1. 3
Looks for a \fBplaywright.config.ts\fR (or \fB.js\fR/\fB.mjs\fR) in the workspace root, then in each repo worktree under it (\fB~/workspace/<feature>/<repo>/\fR). There is no fallback to the Werkmeister root.
.IP 2. 3
If none is found, runs nothing and exits non\-zero. The message lists each workspace service's test command and \fBvalidate:\fR guidance. Libraries, CLIs and extensions are validated that way, not with Playwright; see Test commands and validation guidance.
.IP 3. 3
Otherwise sets \fBSANDBOX_FEATURE\fR and \fBWERKMEISTER_SLOT\fR for the Playwright process and runs \fBnpx playwright test\fR from that directory with a 10\-minute timeout \" name-check: ignore
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
# Run all tests
werkmeister test my\-feature

# Run specific test file
werkmeister test my\-feature \-\- tests/homepage.spec.ts

# Run with headed browser
werkmeister test my\-feature \-\- \-\-headed
.RE
.fi
.PP
The feature doesn't need to have an active slot — if it doesn't, the command warns but proceeds with just the feature header routing.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwerkmeister\fR(1)
